WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.034 --> 00:00:02.168
The 2024 Republican

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.937
presidential field gets a second entry

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.839
this week, and several other prospects

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.140
are taking important steps

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.641
toward candidacies as well.

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:08.975
The former

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:09.843
South Carolina governor

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:10.910
Nikki Haley, is scheduled

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.979
to declare her intentions on Wednesday.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.314
Haley, you might recall

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:15.148
she was Donald Trump's

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.117
first ambassador to the United Nations,

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:18.451
will be the only declared

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:19.386
Republican candidate.

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:21.054
Other than her former boss.

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:21.788
Trump so far,

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.490
anyway, seems more concerned

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.791
about the Florida governor.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.493
Florida Governor Ron DeSantis,

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.360
who is pulling together

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.294
his own campaign team.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:30.230
Trump has repeatedly disparaged

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.498
DeSantis in recent weeks

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:32.966
and the New York Times reports.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:33.967
One big question,

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.602
as DeSantis gears up

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:36.803
is whether and when

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.438
to return Trump's fire.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:39.506
This from that story

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:40.507
quote, For months, Mr.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:41.941
DeSantis has pursued

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.943
a strategy of conflict avoidance,

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.046
but now he faces the pressing question

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:48.114
of how long this approach can work.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:48.348
Mr.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.350
Trump has spent weeks trying to goad Mr.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.618
DeSantis into a fight

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:52.018
with rude

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:54.421
nicknames like Ron the Sanctimonious,

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:56.456
a great reporter back with us.

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:57.490
That story also notes

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.158
now he's calling it

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.293
Meatball as well, apparently.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.461
It is interesting.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:02.228
Trump has

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.063
posted a couple of things

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.531
about Nikki Haley saying she promised

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:05.899
not to run if he was running.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:06.933
She's disloyal,

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.601
but he seems much more concerned

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.103
about Ron DeSantis.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:11.371
And if you look at the polling,

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.173
this is cited in FiveThirtyEight

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.175
in The New York Times today as well.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.177
Ron DeSantis in the thirties ranks

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.511
pretty good historically.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.013
If you look at candidates

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.181
early in the race,

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.183
especially a guy who's never run before.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:23.750
Yep.

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.386
And and if you talk to voters

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:27.520
and you say,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.321
who do you want to be

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.356
the Republican nominee?

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.492
They often say Ron DeSantis.

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.160
If they if they expressed discomfort

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.061
with Trump, you say, okay,

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.062
then who are you looking at?

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.230
Ron DeSantis,

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.499
the name Rolls off of Voters Tongues.

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:40.800
At this point,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.102
I will say that it is

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:43.570
an interesting challenge.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:44.571
Hillary Clinton,

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.873
when she ran against Trump,

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.042
often responded to his daily slights

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:49.943
and controversies.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:50.844
And in some ways,

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:51.778
it was the only way

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.613
that she could get any headlines at all

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.548
was to respond to him.

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:57.050
And her policy message

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.985
got completely lost.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:00.753
Joe Biden took a different tack.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:03.123
He really did not engage Trump

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.390
in the day to day insult.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.526
He just didn't go there.

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:07.627
And, you know,

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.062
the coronavirus pandemic

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.363
sort of helped with that,

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.798
enabling him to ignore Trump.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.066
But he did.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.468
And that obviously worked out

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:16.369
better for him.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.404
But a

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.839
primary is a different

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:21.508
beast than a general election.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.375
And you've got to figure.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.577
Ron DeSantis is looking at all

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:25.778
of the wasteland

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.347
of candidates from 20, 15

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.681
and so this is from

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:30.150
I pose the question at this table.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.384
I know this as well as is Ron

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.619
DeSantis the next Donald Trump

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:33.720
or is he the next Scott Walker.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.555
If you roll back the tape to 2014 Scott

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.523
Walker was very popular

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.324
getting a ton of attention on Fox News

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:39.359
came out ran for president

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.294
and it was not successful.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.028
The polling graph

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:42.495
we showed you

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:44.597
DeSantis has very high numbers

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:45.632
for this early

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.100
in a campaign goes back to you know

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.902
Ronald Reagan Barack Obama

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.870
Ted Kennedy very well no names there.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.172
So that's a good number for him

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.439
which is why Nate Cohn

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.040
smartly writes

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.275
the New York Times say what sets Mr.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.276
DeSantis apart from Mr.

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:57.377
Walker to be blunt,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.444
how many people already say

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.479
they want him to be president?

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.948
Overall, the center says, 32% support.

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:02.515
Mr.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:03.249
Walker, in contrast,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.551
was in single digits at 7%.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:07.387
So there is a difference in the numbers.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.422
The numbers can still be very different

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.890
from standing on a debate stage

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:11.925
where forgive me,

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:13.760
forgive me, but Trump, you know,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.262
little Marco Lion, Ted, low energy, Jeb

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.797
He's a human chainsaw.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:18.097
Yeah.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.966
And right now, there doesn't appear to be

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:20.867
that much daylight

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:22.502
between Trump and DeSantis.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.470
I mean, policy wise,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.239
they lean very to the right

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:27.106
to Santa, sometimes

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.741
more to the right than Trump.

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.943
But one area

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:30.910
we were just talking

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.178
about, Social Security

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.012
and Medicare,

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:33.613
one area

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.949
that Trump hit a lot of his Republican

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:37.850
challengers on

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:38.918
in 2016 was

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.520
Social Security and Medicare saying

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.121
I'm not going to touch this at all,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:45.625
which was a departure from the GOP policy

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:46.593
point at the time.

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:47.760
And that's something that he's

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.728
probably going to hit

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.997
DeSantis on again, as well as a number,

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:51.764
as well as Haley,

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:52.765
as well as a lot of these other

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.400
Republican potential challengers,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.536
because they all have a history

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:57.870
of voting for cuts

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.404
or changes

00:03:58.404 --> 00:03:59.505
to Medicare and Social Security.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.241
And so the governors were in town

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:02.642
this past weekend

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:03.910
included Republicans and Democrats.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:04.477
Spencer Cox

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:06.212
is the new Republican governor of Utah.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.981
It's clear he doesn't want Trump.

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:09.182
The question is, who should it be?

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.517
He likes governors

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:13.219
Sununu.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:15.688
The answer to Santas caution record.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:17.423
Ace is a good friend.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:18.558
Nikki Haley. All of those guys.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:19.859
Governor, former governor.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:21.227
Yeah, I would love a governor

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:21.995
over anybody

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:23.763
that serves in that building

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.965
across the street every day of the week.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.402
It used to be Ronald Reagan.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:30.270
Bill Clinton used to be

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.237
we got governors, right.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:32.939
But then we had Barack Obama

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:33.740
with Senator

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.807
Barack Obama, Senator Joe

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:35.675
Biden, the former vice

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.844
president to Donald Trump, businessman

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:38.611
a lot of Republicans

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:39.312
who don't want Trump

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:40.647
are counting on one of these governors

00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:41.547
to get traction.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:41.881
Right.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.150
There's a lot of built in benefits

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.618
from being a governor, right?

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.387
In a way, just by being governor

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:49.322
that is campaigning in itself.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:50.657
You're able to hold daily

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:51.624
press conferences

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.059
about what you're working on.

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:55.495
And so I understand why

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:57.730
why Spencer Cox is making

00:04:58.131 --> 00:04:59.332
that recommendation

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:00.933
or wants to see a governor

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.935
ascend to be Republican nominee.

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.470
But this is also why

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.439
Governor DeSantis doesn't really have to,

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:08.241
I think, engage in this way

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.809
with Trump right now.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:10.943
He is the governor.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.146
He he can afford to have

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:14.981
this strategy of conflict

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:16.516
avoidance as much as possible

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:17.850
because right now,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:18.918
he doesn't have to sort of

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.887
get in the mud with Trump

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.622
based on the position he holds.

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.090
It served him incredibly well so far.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.291
The question is, does it last well?

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:26.726
And he's been able to get in the mud

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:28.594
with Mickey Mouse and,

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.164
you know, one culture war after another,

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:31.664
after another

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:32.231
after another,

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.132
making headlines

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:34.901
and raising those numbers of voters

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:35.435
who recognize

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:36.769
and I don't think those serve him

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.037
well in a general election

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:40.406
if he does clear the Republican primary.

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:41.808
But it certainly works

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.177
with the base voters at the moment.

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:44.944
The question will be

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:46.646
when do we get four or five Republicans

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:47.580
around the table like this

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.448
or standing on a stage?

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:50.016
And that will get interesting.

